4 Materials and methods
All of the analyses were performed within three computing environments: the Curta High Performance Computing (HPC) system, from the Freie Universität Berlin (1); our lab server, Darwin, a Dell Technologies model PowerEdge R940xa station with four 20-core, 40-thread Intel Xeon Gold 6230 processors and 64 gigabytes (GB) of random access memory (RAM); and Sagarana, the HPC system of the Institute of Biological Sciences (ICB) of the Federal University of Minas Gerais (UFMG).
4.1 Samples
All samples were sequenced, analysed and published in previous works (2, 3). Direct access to the quality-trimmed FASTQ files for all samples, as well as the assembled reference genomes (unpublished) was provided by Dr. Larissa Arantes and Dr. Camila Mazzoni from the Berlin Centre for Genomics in Biodiversity Research (BeGenDiv), through personal communication. In brief, the sample pool is comprised of individuals identified as being from one of four species of sea turtles: the loggerhead sea turtle Caretta caretta, the hawksbill Eretmochelys imbricata, the green turtle Chelonia mydas and the olive ridley sea turtle Lepidochelys olivacea; and their suspected hybrids. This identification, when present, was done morphologically through observation of the carapace features of the sampled individuals, but was not present for all samples, e. g. the stillborn hatchlings from the Abrolhos Archipelago (2). In total, 116 samples were sequenced by Arantes (2) using the 3RAD methodology (3rad method citation) and 158 samples were sequenced by Arantes and Vilaça (3) using the double-digest restriction-site associated DNA (ddRAD) sequencing methodology (ddRAD method citation). This amounts to a total of 274 sequencing samples, of which 
